---
import { getImage } from 'astro:assets';
import audienceImage from '../../../src/assets/img/pexels-karola-g-5908758.webp';

const zinInputs = await getImage({ src: audienceImage, format: 'webp', quality: 85 });
---
  <section class="sec sec-light">
    <div class="container">

      <div class="row">
        <div class="col-md-8 pe-1">
          <span class="zin-eyebrow">Who Zin is for</span>
          <h2 class="zin-h2 mb-4">For people who want more from their own thinking.</h2>

          <p class="zin-body mb-2"><i class="bi bi-check orange-text me-2"></i>
            People who keep losing the ideas that matter most. 
          </p>

        <p class="zin-body mb-2"><i class="bi bi-check orange-text me-2"></i>
          People who have been writing and thinking for years and know their content has value.
        </p>

        <p class="zin-body mb-2"><i class="bi bi-check orange-text me-2"></i>
          People who use AI and want the output to sound real.
        </p>

        <p class="zin-body mb-2"><i class="bi bi-check orange-text me-2"></i>
          People who want to think more clearly, remember more reliably and build on what they already know.
        </p>

<p class="zin-body mb-2 fw-semibold">
Zin is powerful if you publish regularly, work with clients, or build your credibility through what you know.
</p>

<!-- <p class="zin-body mb-2">
Zin is <b>not for everyone</b>. If you want AI to do the thinking for you, Zin will disappoint you. 
</p>

<p class="zin-body mb-2">
And Zin is <b>not a publishing suite</b>. It captures, organises and assembles. 
</p> -->

          

        </div>

        
      <div class="col-12 col-md-4">

         <img
          src={audienceImage.src}
          alt="Zin — lack of capture is the problem"
          class="solution-img"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />

      </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>